    "done_when": "Measure where a production pick spends its time, so it can stay inside its 5 s bound. Change nothing in the code.\n\nBackground: on 2026-10-07, 10 of 79 picks fell back on timeout. The last three dispatches from session s25-picker all fell back, for example run r-20261007T160947007052-catalogue-declares-lanes. Jev itself answered in 0.46 s median and 0.71 s at the 90th percentile, but the whole pick took 1.9 s median and 5.0 s at the 90th percentile.\n\n1. Instrument a pick out-of-process. Use a scratch copy of the tree under the run directory, or cProfile around reckon.crew.dispatch.dispatch_picker_selection with a stub Jev client that answers at once. Run it against project reckon's real state on this host. Time each stage separately:\n   - building the records and the verdict inputs;\n   - the budget snapshot;\n   - lane_context.build, including lane.json, paid-lanes.json and the ledger reads;\n   - snapshot.candidates, including the per-candidate context-fit estimate and the authority resolution;\n   - prompt rendering;\n   - the Jev call.\n2. Repeat each measurement at least five times, warm and cold, and report the median and the maximum per stage.\n3. Name the two or three stages that put a pick over 5 s. For each one, give the call path down to file:line and a proposed change with its expected saving. Mark which proposals would edit reckon/crew/dispatch.py or reckon/cli.py, since those two files are on hold for a module split.\n\nDeliver a report.md and a timings.json in your declared report directory, plus a short figure: per-stage median and maximum, horizontal bars, data-ink style. A negative control shows the instrument can see a slow stage: inject a 2 s sleep into one stage in the scratch copy and show it in the timings.",
